        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A new home treadmill typically costs between about $400 and $2,000, with walking pads below
          that range and premium and commercial machines well above it. The spread is wide because
          "treadmill" covers several genuinely different machines, and the price tracks the motor,
          the frame and the warranty far more than it tracks the screen.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Short Answer: How Much Is a Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            As typical US ranges: a <strong>walking pad</strong> costs roughly $200 to $600, a
            <strong>budget folding treadmill</strong> roughly $400 to $900, a
            <strong>mid-range home treadmill</strong> roughly $1,000 to $2,000, a
            <strong>premium home treadmill</strong> roughly $2,000 to $4,000, and a
            <strong>commercial treadmill</strong> of the kind you find in a gym roughly $4,000 to
            $10,000 or more. Curved non-motorized trainers sit in their own bracket, which we cover
            separately below.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most people who buy a treadmill for regular home use, and keep using it, end up
            spending somewhere between $1,000 and $2,000. That is not because cheaper machines are
            useless — many are fine for walking — but because that is the band where motors rated
            for continuous duty, decks long enough to run on, and warranties measured in years
            rather than months start to appear together.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whether someone asks how much a treadmill is, how much a running machine costs or how
            much treadmills cost in general, the honest answer is the same: it depends on which of
            these machines you mean. The rest of this page is about what the money buys at each
            step, and what you will spend on top of the sticker price. If you want help deciding
            what <em>you</em> should spend rather than what the market charges, that is a different
            question, and our
            <a href="/how-much-should-you-spend-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">framework for setting a treadmill budget</a>
            answers it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Treadmill Prices by Tier</h2>
<p>
            The tiers below are deliberately broad. Within each one you will find machines that
            punch above their price and machines coasting on a brand name, but the band tells you
            what to expect as a baseline.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Walking pads and under-desk treadmills: about $200-$600.</strong> No
            handrails or minimal ones, a short deck, a small motor and a top speed usually around
            4 mph. Built for walking, often while working.</li>
            <li><strong>Budget folding treadmills: about $400-$900.</strong> Full treadmills with
            handrails and a console, typically with a modest motor, a deck that is often on the
            short side for running, limited or manual incline, and short parts warranties.</li>
            <li><strong>Mid-range home treadmills: about $1,000-$2,000.</strong> Continuous-duty
            motors around 3 CHP, decks around 20 by 60 inches, powered incline, sturdier frames
            and multi-year warranties on parts. This is the band most regular runners land in.</li>
            <li><strong>Premium home treadmills: about $2,000-$4,000.</strong> Heavier frames,
            larger rollers, quieter and stiffer decks, more incline range (sometimes decline), and
            often a large touchscreen tied to a subscription service.</li>
            <li><strong>Commercial treadmills: about $4,000-$10,000+.</strong> Machines built for
            hours of daily use by many different people, with AC motors, heavy steel frames and
            service networks. Light-commercial models occupy the lower end; full gym units with
            premium consoles go well past $10,000.</li>
            <li><strong>Curved non-motorized treadmills: typically several thousand dollars.</strong>
            Self-powered, with a slatted curved surface you drive with your own stride. Priced
            closer to commercial machines than to home ones.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The jump between tiers is not linear in quality. The step from $600 to $1,200 usually
            buys a bigger improvement in how long a machine lasts than the step from $2,000 to
            $3,000, which tends to buy refinement, features and software. We look at that
            trade-off in more detail in
            <a href="/cheap-vs-premium-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cheap vs. premium treadmills</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Walking Pads: $200 to $600</h2>
<p>
            Walking pads are the cheapest way into a motorized belt, and for their intended job —
            steady walking, often under a standing desk — they can be good value. What you are
            buying is a compact deck, a small motor and a slim profile that slides under a sofa
            or bed.
          </p>
          <p>
            What you are not buying is a running machine. Most cap out around 4 mph, some fold up
            a handrail to reach a little more, and the short deck and small motor mean that
            treating one as a cheap treadmill for jogging tends to end badly for the machine. At
            the lower end of the range, warranties can be short and replacement parts hard to find,
            so the effective cost per year of use is higher than the sticker suggests.
          </p>
          <p>
            If walking is genuinely all you plan to do, spending at the upper end of the walking
            pad range usually buys a quieter motor, a slightly longer deck and better support. Our
            guide to
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmills</a>
            covers what separates the good ones, and
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad vs. treadmill</a>
            covers when a pad stops being enough.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget-folding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Budget Folding Treadmills: $400 to $900</h2>
<p>
            This is the tier where most first-time buyers start looking, and where expectations
            most often need adjusting. Machines here are real treadmills: handrails, a console,
            usually some incline, and a top speed around 10 mph. For walking and occasional light
            jogging by a lighter user, a well-chosen one can serve for years.
          </p>
          <p>
            The compromises are predictable. Motors tend to be rated by peak rather than continuous
            horsepower, which flatters the number. Decks are frequently shorter than the roughly
            60 inches most runners need, frames flex more, and parts warranties can be as short as
            a year. Under regular running, it is the motor controller and drive components that
            give out first, and a repair can cost a large fraction of what the machine did.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of that makes the tier a mistake. It makes it a tier for a specific kind of use.
            Our roundup of the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $500</a>
            is candid about which machines in this band are worth it and for whom.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mid-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Mid-Range Treadmills: $1,000 to $2,000</h2>
<p>
            This is where a treadmill stops being a walking machine you can occasionally jog on
            and becomes something you can train on. The typical spec sheet in this band reads like
            the minimum serious runners are told to look for: a continuous-duty motor around
            3 CHP, a deck around 20 by 60 inches, powered incline to roughly 10 to 15 percent, and
            a top speed of 12 mph.
          </p>
          <p>
            Just as important is what does not show up in a spec table. Frames are heavier and
            stiffer, rollers are larger (which is easier on the belt and motor), and warranties on
            the motor and frame stretch to many years or lifetime, with parts typically covered for
            several. That warranty structure is the manufacturer telling you how long it expects
            the machine to last.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you run more than once or twice a week, or there is more than one regular user in
            the house, this is usually the tier to start in. Our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $1,000</a>
            covers the machines at the bottom edge of this band, which is where the best value
            usually sits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="premium" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Premium Home Treadmills: $2,000 to $4,000</h2>
<p>
            Above about $2,000 the improvements become less about durability and more about
            refinement. You get heavier, quieter frames, wider incline ranges and sometimes decline,
            better deck cushioning, faster incline motors, and in many cases a large touchscreen
            that streams classes.
          </p>
          <p>
            That screen is where a lot of the price goes, and it often comes with a catch: much of
            what makes it useful sits behind a monthly subscription. A premium machine bought for
            its screen is a purchase you keep paying for, which we come back to under hidden costs.
          </p>
          <p>
            Premium machines are worth it for heavy users, for tall or heavier runners who benefit
            from a longer deck and stiffer frame, and for people who will genuinely use the
            interactive content. They are poor value for someone who mostly walks. Our page on
            <a href="/treadmills-over-2500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills over $2,500</a>
            looks at where the extra money goes at the top of this band.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="commercial-curved" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Commercial and Curved Treadmills: $4,000 and Up</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Commercial treadmills</strong> are designed for gyms, hotels and clinics, where
            a machine might run for many hours a day under users of every size and gait. They use
            heavier frames, AC drive motors, commercial-grade belts and decks, and come with
            service arrangements a home buyer rarely needs. Light-commercial models, aimed at
            small facilities and serious home users, tend to start around $4,000; full gym units
            with premium consoles commonly cost $8,000 to $10,000 or more.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a home, a commercial machine is usually more than you need. The exceptions are
            households with several heavy users, very high weekly mileage, or someone who simply
            wants a machine that will outlast them. A used or refurbished commercial unit can make
            sense here, which we cover below. Our
            <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial treadmills guide</a>
            explains what the commercial label actually guarantees.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Curved non-motorized treadmills</strong> have no motor at all: you push a slatted,
            curved running surface with your own stride, and speed rises as you move toward the
            front. They are popular for sprint and interval training and priced in the thousands
            because the slat belt and bearing system are expensive to build. The much cheaper flat
            manual treadmills, often under a few hundred dollars, are a completely different and
            far more limited machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="peloton" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. How Much Is a Peloton Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            The Peloton Tread sits in the premium tier described above. We are deliberately not
            quoting a figure, because Peloton has changed its treadmill pricing several times since
            launch, through permanent price changes, bundles and frequent promotions, and any
            number printed here would be wrong for some of the people reading it. Check Peloton's
            own site for the current price, and look at whether delivery and setup are included,
            since that has varied too.
          </p>
          <p>
            The sticker price is only part of the cost. Most of the Tread's classes and programmes
            require an All-Access membership, billed monthly, and without it the machine offers a
            much more limited experience. Over several years the membership can add up to a
            substantial share of the original purchase price, so the fair comparison is machine
            plus membership over the time you expect to own it, not machine against machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Peloton has also sold a larger slatted-belt model, the Tread+, which has been priced
            above the Tread. If you like the idea of class-led running but not a mandatory
            subscription, there are capable machines that work without one; our list of the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            is a reasonable place to start comparing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hidden-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. The Hidden Costs Beyond the Sticker Price</h2>
<p>
            The price on the product page is rarely what a treadmill costs you. These are the
            additions to budget for, as typical figures.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Delivery and assembly.</strong> Many online sellers deliver curbside only,
            leaving a box that can weigh well over 200 lb at your door. Room-of-choice delivery
            and professional assembly are often extras, commonly in the region of $150 to $300
            together. Specialty dealers more often include both in the price, which narrows the
            gap with an online deal more than it first appears.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Subscriptions.</strong> Many touchscreen treadmills are designed around an app
            membership. The arithmetic is unforgiving: a subscription of $40 a month is $480 a year
            and $2,400 over five years, which can exceed the cost of the treadmill itself. Check
            what the machine does without the subscription before you buy, not after.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A mat.</strong> A treadmill mat protects the floor, reduces dust drawn into the
            motor compartment and damps some vibration. Expect to spend somewhere around $40 to
            $100 for one sized for a full treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Maintenance.</strong> Routine upkeep — silicone lubricant, a cleaning cloth, the
            occasional belt adjustment — is cheap, roughly $15 to $40 a year according to our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance cost breakdown</a>.
            Skipping it is what makes the expensive repairs, such as motors, belts and controller
            boards, arrive sooner.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Electricity.</strong> Smaller than people fear. A home treadmill typically draws
            a few hundred watts in use, and for most households that works out to a few dollars a
            month. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-electricity-usage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill electricity guide</a>
            shows the arithmetic, including standby draw.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Extended warranties.</strong> Often offered at checkout. On a machine that
            already carries a long manufacturer warranty they rarely add much; on a budget machine
            with a one-year parts warranty they can be worth reading, but read what they exclude.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-prices" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. How Much Do Used Treadmills Cost?</h2>
<p>
            Treadmills depreciate steeply and then level off. As a typical pattern, a well-kept
            machine sells for roughly half its original price after a couple of years and about a
            third after three to five, after which the price flattens because what remains is
            mostly the value of the frame and motor.
          </p>
          <p>
            That makes used buying attractive at the mid-range and premium tiers, where a
            two-to-four-year-old machine can deliver a far better frame and motor than the same
            money buys new. It is much less attractive at the budget end, where a used machine
            has already consumed a chunk of a short life and the savings in dollars are small.
          </p>
          <p>
            The trade-offs are that manufacturer warranties often do not transfer to a second
            owner, you usually have to collect and move a very heavy object yourself, and wear is
            not always visible. Refurbished commercial units from specialist resellers sit in
            between: more expensive than private sales, but often inspected and sold with a short
            warranty. Our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new vs. used treadmill guide</a>
            and the
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill buying checklist</a>
            cover what to inspect before you hand over cash.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-drives-price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. What Actually Drives the Price</h2>
<p>
            If two treadmills look similar and one costs twice as much, the difference usually
            sits in one of five places.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The motor.</strong> A continuous-duty rating is what predicts whether a motor
            survives regular use; peak figures are marketing. Larger continuous-duty motors cost
            more to build and run cooler under load. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower guide</a>
            explains how to read the ratings.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The frame and deck.</strong> Heavier steel, a thicker deck and larger rollers
            make a machine quieter, stiffer and longer-lived, and they are expensive. Deck size
            matters too: a running-length deck costs more than a walking-length one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline and decline.</strong> Powered incline adds a motor and mechanism; wide
            incline ranges and decline add more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The console.</strong> A large touchscreen with streaming can add a considerable
            amount to the price, and it is the component most likely to feel dated first.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The warranty.</strong> Long motor, frame and parts coverage costs the
            manufacturer money, and it is priced in. That is not a bad thing: a long parts and
            labour warranty is one of the more reliable signals that a machine is built to last.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Paying Less for the Same Machine</h2>
<p>
            The price of a given treadmill is not fixed. Fitness equipment tends to be discounted
            around the major retail sales and is often under the most price pressure after the
            new-year rush, when demand has peaked and retailers are clearing stock. Our guide to
            the
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best time of year to buy a treadmill</a>
            goes through the calendar.
          </p>
          <p>
            Beyond timing, compare the full delivered-and-assembled price rather than the sticker,
            check whether last year's model of the same machine is still on sale for less, and ask
            specialty dealers whether they have floor models. The savings from any of these are
            often larger than the difference between two competing machines in the same tier. If
            you are hoping to pay with health-account money, read
            <a href="/are-treadmills-hsa-fsa-eligible/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">are treadmills HSA or FSA eligible</a>
            first: the retailer label does not decide it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much is a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As typical US ranges, a walking pad costs about $200 to $600, a budget folding treadmill about $400 to $900, a mid-range home treadmill about $1,000 to $2,000, a premium home machine about $2,000 to $4,000, and a commercial gym treadmill about $4,000 to $10,000 or more. Most regular home users end up spending between $1,000 and $2,000.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a running machine cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A machine genuinely suited to regular running — a continuous-duty motor around 3 CHP, a deck around 20 by 60 inches and a 12 mph top speed — typically starts around $1,000 new. Cheaper machines can handle occasional light jogging, but their motors and short decks are usually built with walking in mind.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much is a Peloton treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Peloton Tread sits in the premium home treadmill tier. Peloton has changed the price several times, so check its site for the current figure and whether delivery is included. Budget for the All-Access membership too, since most of the Tread's classes require it and it adds a meaningful amount over several years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much do treadmills cost to run each month?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In electricity, usually only a few dollars a month for typical home use, since most home treadmills draw a few hundred watts while running. The bigger recurring cost, if your machine has one, is a content subscription, which can run to hundreds of dollars a year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much are used treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As a typical pattern, a well-kept treadmill sells for roughly half its original price after about two years and roughly a third after three to five years. Used buying offers the best value in the mid-range and premium tiers; at the budget end the savings are small and the remaining life is short.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cheap treadmills worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For walking and occasional light use by one person, a well-chosen budget treadmill or walking pad can be. For regular running, or a household with several users, cheaper machines tend to wear out quickly and repairs can cost a large share of the purchase price, which usually makes a mid-range machine cheaper per year of use.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Comparing Treadmill Prices</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most overspending and underspending on treadmills comes from comparing the wrong numbers. These are the four we see most.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing sticker prices instead of delivered prices</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A machine that is $200 cheaper online can cost the same or more once curbside delivery is upgraded to room-of-choice and assembly is added. Always compare the price of the treadmill standing assembled in the room where it will live.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the subscription</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A touchscreen treadmill whose best features need a monthly membership has a five-year cost far above its sticker price. Work out what the machine does without the membership, and add the subscription over your expected ownership to the purchase price before comparing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying budget for running</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A budget machine bought for regular running often fails within a few years, and replacing it costs more than buying the right tier once. If you will run more than occasionally, the cheapest machine is frequently the most expensive option per year.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying premium for walking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The opposite error. A walker does not need a 4 CHP motor, a 22-inch deck or a 15 mph top speed. Spending on those buys capacity that will never be used; a solid mid-range or good walking-focused machine serves the same purpose for far less.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

